  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nWe need to find an improved step function h that minimizes the maximum correlation overlap. The current algorithm uses floating-point optimization, but it's stuck due to local optima. We'll use a combination of: (1) a multi-resolution strategy to escape local optima, (2) a novel interval merging/dispersal technique, and (3) a directed descent for refinement. This explores different dimensions while retaining the parent's structure.\n\nMathematical specification:\nWe represent h as an array of floating points with:\n- h[i] \u2208 [0,1]\n- sum(h) \u2248 n/2 (for n samples)\nThe objective: minimize C5 = max_k (np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full') * dx\nConstraints are enforced via clipping and projection.\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Start with 1-h symmetry to get free init.\n2. For trial sizes from 200 to 800 steps:\n     - Use the parent solution but rescaled to new n\n     - Add a coarse incenter/outcenter Necula structure: identify intervals of high mass and split/duplicate them strategically\n     - Use gradient projection with adaptive penalty\n3. Phase 1: Linear ascent on coarse grid every 20 seconds\n4. Phase 2: Directed Noise Descent (Gradients + Noise)\n5. Phase 3: Refining based on local curvature\n6. Final polishing\n\nSearch choices:\n- Trial sizes: [300,400,600,800]\n- Symmetry: always apply 1-h after each full optimization\n- Tolerance: 1e-7 for floating point\n- Polishing: 50 steps of projected gradient\n\nBudget and robustness:\n- Total time: 1100s, check every 200s\n- Use memoization for objective calculations per trial size\n- If stuck, resample around the best candidate\n\nValidation and return: \n1. After each run, project candidate to [0,1] and sum=1\n2. Recompute C5 with original evaluator\n3. Select minimum C5 found",
